<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Should We Preserve in the Future?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain general concepts in simple terms. Text: Summarize information from instructions or announcements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the importance of heritage sites to classmates, using simple language and a map or image of a heritage site to emphasize cultural value (e.g., shows a timeline of restoration efforts and explains, "Casco Viejo is important because it represents Panama's colonial past and restoration efforts.")
                Can summarize instructions for a field trip to a historical site, ensuring classmates understand the objectives by using visual aids (e.g., shows a timeline of events and says, "We'll learn about Panama's history and see how it connects to today.")
                Can clarify the purpose of cultural preservation by using bilingual explanations and visuals in peer discussions (e.g., "Preserving our heritage sites helps us remember and share our history," with a translated summary if needed).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of two significant Panamanian heritage sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, the Panama Canal). Students work in pairs to briefly explain what they know about each site and its importance in their own words. The teacher prompts with "Why do you think preserving these places is so important for our history and identity?" Students use vocabulary like 'heritage', 'history', 'identity', 'preserve'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a general concept simply. For example, the teacher explains, "Cultural heritage is basically everything we inherit from the past that we want to preserve for the future. It's like our family's old photos or stories, but for a whole country. It helps us remember who we are and where we came from." The teacher also models summarizing a short instruction: "If an announcement says, 'All visitors must stay on marked paths,' I would explain it simply as, 'Please walk only where the signs tell you, to protect the site.'" The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /eɪ/ in 'great' and 'say' when modeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of mediation:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I mediate, do I just repeat exactly what I hear, or do I rephrase it to make it easier to understand?" (Expected answer: Rephrase it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the goal of mediation to confuse people or to clarify information?" (Expected answer: Clarify.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents an official-looking announcement regarding the "Heritage Virtual Exhibition" project, outlining its purpose, key phases, and general instructions. The announcement includes details about choosing heritage 'sites', the importance of 'restoration efforts', and the 'objectives' of the exhibition. It mentions "The Panama Canal opened in 1914, which became a pivotal part of Panama's global identity..." and "Students will continue to research historical sites." (See Appendix for sample announcement text.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Explain general concepts in simple terms. Summarize information from instructions or announcements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this announcement?" (To inform students about the virtual exhibition project.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Questions:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the announcement, why is creating this virtual exhibition important?" (To showcase Panama's cultural heritage and appreciate preservation efforts.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the initial steps mentioned for developing the exhibition?" (Selecting a heritage site, researching its history, and outlining preservation efforts.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When did the Panama Canal open, and what did it become?" (It opened in 1914, becoming a pivotal part of Panama's global identity.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice explaining general concepts and summarizing information. Focus on using relative clauses ('which', 'where'), past simple vs. present perfect (e.g., 'opened' vs. 'has attracted'), future simple ('will continue'), and vocabulary: 'heritage', 'history', 'sites', 'culture', 'restoration', 'legacy', 'identity', 'efforts', 'preserve', 'attract', 'became', 'continue', 'restore', 'important', 'historical', 'cultural', 'significant'. Pronunciation practice of /eɪ/ and reduced vowels in 'important' /ˈɪmˈpɔː.tənt/ and 'heritage' /ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Sentence Mediation (Individual then Pair Check):</b> The teacher provides sentences containing relative clauses or past simple/present perfect about heritage sites. Students rewrite them in simpler terms for a younger audience, then compare with a partner.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, attracts tourists from around the world." <br>Simplified: "Casco Viejo is a very special place that many tourists like to visit. It’s important because it is part of our global heritage." (Focus on 'which', 'attracts' and simple explanation.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Panama Canal opened in 1914, transforming global trade." <br>Simplified: "The Panama Canal started working in 1914, and it changed how countries do business across the world." (Focus on past simple 'opened', vocabulary 'transformed'/'changed'.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Instruction Summarization (Small Groups):</b> Students work in groups of three. The teacher provides a set of complex instructions for a hypothetical field trip to a historical site. Each group must summarize these instructions into 3-4 bullet points using simple language, ensuring clarity. For example, original instruction: "Participants are required to adhere strictly to the designated pathways and refrain from any unauthorized interaction with the historical artifacts." Group mediation: "We must stay on the paths to protect the historical items." They also practice asking, "Why is preserving historic landmarks like Casco Viejo important?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Practice: Concept Clarification (Pair Dialogue):</b> Students in pairs take turns explaining the concept of "cultural preservation" using their own words and relevant vocabulary (e.g., 'legacy', 'efforts', 'relevant', 'traditions'). One student explains, the other asks clarifying questions. They practice discussing heritage respectfully. E.g., "Preserving our heritage sites helps us remember and share our history, and keep our traditions alive."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will consolidate their learning by performing a mediation task centered on the "Heritage Virtual Exhibition" project. They will work in their exhibition project groups (3-4 students).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Each group prepares a 3-minute "Virtual Exhibition Overview and Clarification Session" to be presented to another peer group (acting as a "junior" class or "new team members").</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Explain Cultural Significance:</b> Groups will explain the importance of their chosen heritage site(s) for the virtual exhibition, using simple language and at least one map or image as a visual aid. They must emphasize its 'cultural value' and 'legacy', detailing any 'restoration efforts' they have researched. They must use the present perfect to describe ongoing preservation efforts (e.g., "Our team has preserved detailed records of..."). For example: "Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is important because it represents Panama's colonial past and the amazing restoration efforts that have transformed it." They also practice the pronunciation of 'important' and 'heritage'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Summarize Project Instructions:</b> Groups will then summarize the main instructions or objectives for creating their virtual exhibition, ensuring the 'junior' class understands the next steps. They should use future simple for predictions and plans (e.g., "We will continue to add more interactive features next week."). They are encouraged to use bilingual explanations if needed for clarity ("Preserving our heritage sites helps us remember and share our history," with a translated summary if needed).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 3: Q&A and Clarification:</b> The presenting group answers questions from the "junior" class, clarifying any doubts about the heritage site or the project instructions, ensuring everyone grasps the purpose of cultural preservation. They will demonstrate sociolinguistic competence by discussing heritage respectfully and showing interest in cultural diversity.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a checklist/rubric focused on mediation skills and peer assessment during the Q&A.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' "Virtual Exhibition Overview and Clarification Session" (from Stage 4) will be assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of explanations about heritage sites' significance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy and completeness of summarized project instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visual aids (maps/images) to enhance understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and accurate use of target grammar: relative clauses, past simple vs. present perfect, future simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Consistent use of target vocabulary related to heritage and preservation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, particularly of long vowel /eɪ/ and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words ('important', 'heritage').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to questions and clarify information effectively, demonstrating respectful discussion and interest in cultural diversity.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained clearly about our heritage site today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The most challenging part of summarizing the project instructions simply was..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new mediation skills did I practice today, and how can I apply them when working on future projects or explaining things to others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When might I need to explain a complex concept or summarize instructions for someone in my daily life outside of English class?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The groups demonstrated a good grasp of the content and the ability to explain heritage site significance. However, some students still struggled with simplifying the grammar structures, especially when using relative clauses in simplified explanations. More focused practice on rephrasing complex sentences into simpler ones would be beneficial."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The use of visuals greatly aided in the mediation process. I need to ensure all students are consistently encouraged to integrate visuals in future mediation tasks."</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes our theme on "What Should We Preserve in the Future?" and the associated project. In our next unit, we will explore global environmental issues, where the ability to explain complex concepts, summarize information, and mediate understanding across different perspectives will continue to be critical linguistic and 21st-century skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>